 { As we saw in Karpov-Hort, in rook vs. pawn (s) endings the intermediate check is a useful weapon for the stronger side. Here is one more example on this topic. } 1. { -KEY- } Rg7+ $1 { Before approaching with his king, White forces the enemy king to a less fortunate square, thus gaining a vital tempo. } ( { a) In contrast, } 1. Kb7 $2 h5 { leads to a draw, e.g. } 2. Kc6 h4 3. Kd5 h3 4. Ke4 Kg3 5. Ke3 Kg2 $1 ) 
 ( { b) And } 1. Rh7 $2 h5 { is similar. } ) 
Kf4 { Moving the king to the h-file would just obstruct the pawn. } 2. Rh7 Kg5 3. Kb7 { Here Black resigned; let's see a possible conclusion of the game: } h5 4. Kc6 Kg4 5. Kd5 h4 6. Ke4 Kg3 7. Ke3 h3 8. Rg7+ { White wins. } 
 1-0

