 { Of course an intermediate check isn't the best choice on every occasion. Here is one example featuring the misuse of this method.It is clear that Black must soon sacrifice his rook for the d-pawn, but which is the best way to do it? } 1... { -KEY- } Re2+ $2 { A fatal decision. Before placing his rook on d2 Black aims to move the enemy king away from the battle, but the effect of this intermediate check is just the opposite! } ( 1... Rd2 $1 { at once led to a draw; i.e. } 2. d8=Q $11 Rxd8 3. Rxd8 a4 4. Kd5 a3 5. Kd4 Kb3 6. Kd3 Kb2 7. Rb8 Kc1 { a variation that we just saw inExample 181 (Svidler-Bruzón) with reversed flanks. } ) 
2. Kf5 $1 Rd2 3. d8=Q Rxd8 4. Rxd8 a4 5. Ke4 Kc3 ( { Or } 5... a3 6. Kd3 a2 7. Rb8 Ka3 8. Kc2 $1 a1=N 9. Kc3 Ka2 10. Rb7 { etc. } ) 
6. Ra8 Kb3 7. Kd3 a3 8. Rb8+ Ka2 9. Kc3 ( { In view of } 9. Kc3 Ka1 10. Kb3 a2 11. Rh8 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0
